> To the Public Records Unit, Oregon State Police:
>
> This is a request under the Oregon Public Records Law, ORS 192.314. 
> The period for items 1 through 4 is January 1, 2023 to the present.
>
> I request the following.
>
>  1. Every request for a threat assessment made by, or routed through,
>     the Government and Media Relations section, with its date, the
>     agency or person it came from, and the unit or officer it was
>     assigned to.
>  2. Every computer-aided dispatch (CAD) event opened at the request
>     of, or after a referral from, the Government and Media Relations
>     section, with its date and event type.
>  3. Every referral from another state agency to the Government and
>     Media Relations section that the section sent on to the Criminal
>     Investigations Division, with its date, the referring agency, and
>     the unit or officer it was assigned to.
>  4. In the alternative to items 1 through 3, any existing record,
>     report, log, or tracking system that counts or lists them.
>  5. Every policy, directive, general order, procedure, and training
>     material in effect at any time since January 1, 2023 that governs:
>     (a) when the Government and Media Relations section may refer a
>     matter to the Criminal Investigations Division or request a threat
>     assessment;
>     (b) threat assessments of members of the public;
>     (c) “knock and talk” contacts, including any guidance on
>     approaching a residence behind a locked gate, on posted property,
>     or on restricted federal land, or with another agency’s help to
>     gain access.
>
>
> I request records in their native electronic format with metadata 
> intact, delivered electronically. Rolling production is welcome.
>
> For each record withheld or redacted, identify the record, cite the 
> specific exemption relied on, and state whether the public interest 
> balancing that the exemption requires was performed.
>
> *Fee waiver.* I request a full waiver under ORS 192.324(5). Making 
> these records available primarily benefits the general public.
>
>  1. *Identity of the requester.* I publish oprdvolunteerabuse.org, a
>     public archive of government records. Every production I receive
>     is published there in full, as produced, each document at its own
>     permanent address, open to anyone without an account or a fee.
>     This request is not for my own file. It is for the State Police’s
>     practice: every instance of it since January 1, 2023.
>  2. *Purpose.* The purpose is publication. Every record produced will
>     be published in full, unedited, the day it is received. Anyone
>     will be able to see which agencies refer members of the public to
>     the State Police’s public relations office, what those people were
>     accused of, what officers did next, and how each matter ended.
>  3. *Character of the information.* The Government and Media Relations
>     section is the State Police’s press and government relations
>     office. It is not an investigative unit. These records show
>     whether state agencies use that office to send criminal
>     investigators and threat assessments toward members of the public,
>     how often, and against whom. They show whether officers came to
>     those people’s homes, and how many of those matters ended with no
>     crime charged. Item 5 is the written rule for a warrantless
>     approach to a home: when officers may cross a locked gate or
>     posted property, enter restricted federal land, or use another
>     agency to gain access. Every resident of Oregon is subject to
>     these rules.
>  4. *Whether the information is already public.* It is not. No public
>     source reports a single instance of the Government and Media
>     Relations section requesting a threat assessment or referring a
>     member of the public to the Criminal Investigations Division. The
>     instances exist only in the State Police’s own files. If any
>     policy requested under item 5 is already published, identify
>     where, and I will withdraw that part of the request.
>  5. *Ability to disseminate.* The State Police production under
>     PR27478 was published in full on September 3, 2026, the day it was
>     received, at
>     https://oprdvolunteerabuse.org/record/osp/01M1M4WF78XJPEJJ48D1JZ4SJ8/,
>     where it can be read today. Every prior production in my matters
>     has been published the same way.
>
>
> If any part of the fee is not waived, reduce it as ORS 192.324(5) 
> provides, state in writing for each of these five factors why it does 
> or does not weigh in favor of a waiver, and give an itemized estimate 
> by task and hourly rate before any work begins.
>
