        <p>To the Public Records Unit, Oregon State Police:</p>
        <p>This is a request under the Oregon Public Records Law, ORS
          192.314. The period for items 1 through 4 is January 1, 2023
          to the present.</p>
        <p>I request the following.</p>
        <ol start="1">
          <li>Every request for a threat assessment made by, or routed
            through, the Government and Media Relations section, with
            its date, the agency or person it came from, and the unit or
            officer it was assigned to.</li>
          <li>Every computer-aided dispatch (CAD) event opened at the
            request of, or after a referral from, the Government and
            Media Relations section, with its date and event type.</li>
          <li>Every referral from another state agency to the Government
            and Media Relations section that the section sent on to the
            Criminal Investigations Division, with its date, the
            referring agency, and the unit or officer it was assigned
            to.</li>
          <li>In the alternative to items 1 through 3, any existing
            record, report, log, or tracking system that counts or lists
            them.</li>
          <li>Every policy, directive, general order, procedure, and
            training material in effect at any time since January 1,
            2023 that governs:<br>
            (a) when the Government and Media Relations section may
            refer a matter to the Criminal Investigations Division or
            request a threat assessment;<br>
            (b) threat assessments of members of the public;<br>
            (c) “knock and talk” contacts, including any guidance on
            approaching a residence behind a locked gate, on posted
            property, or on restricted federal land, or with another
            agency’s help to gain access.</li>
        </ol>
        <div dir="auto"><br>
        </div>
        <p>I request records in their native electronic format with
          metadata intact, delivered electronically. Rolling production
          is welcome.</p>
        <p>For each record withheld or redacted, identify the record,
          cite the specific exemption relied on, and state whether the
          public interest balancing that the exemption requires was
          performed.</p>
        <p><b>Fee waiver.</b> I request a full waiver under ORS
          192.324(5). Making these records available primarily benefits
          the general public.</p>
        <ol start="1">
          <li><b>Identity of the requester.</b> I publish
            oprdvolunteerabuse.org, a public archive of government
            records. Every production I receive is published there in
            full, as produced, each document at its own permanent
            address, open to anyone without an account or a fee. This
            request is not for my own file. It is for the State Police’s
            practice: every instance of it since January 1, 2023.</li>
          <li><b>Purpose.</b> The purpose is publication. Every record
            produced will be published in full, unedited, the day it is
            received. Anyone will be able to see which agencies refer
            members of the public to the State Police’s public relations
            office, what those people were accused of, what officers did
            next, and how each matter ended.</li>
          <li><b>Character of the information.</b> The Government and
            Media Relations section is the State Police’s press and
            government relations office. It is not an investigative
            unit. These records show whether state agencies use that
            office to send criminal investigators and threat assessments
            toward members of the public, how often, and against whom.
            They show whether officers came to those people’s homes, and
            how many of those matters ended with no crime charged. Item
            5 is the written rule for a warrantless approach to a home:
            when officers may cross a locked gate or posted property,
            enter restricted federal land, or use another agency to gain
            access. Every resident of Oregon is subject to these rules.</li>
          <li><b>Whether the information is already public.</b> It is
            not. No public source reports a single instance of the
            Government and Media Relations section requesting a threat
            assessment or referring a member of the public to the
            Criminal Investigations Division. The instances exist only
            in the State Police’s own files. If any policy requested
            under item 5 is already published, identify where, and I
            will withdraw that part of the request.</li>
          <li><b>Ability to disseminate.</b> The State Police production
            under PR27478 was published in full on September 3, 2026,
            the day it was received, at <a
href="https://oprdvolunteerabuse.org/record/osp/01M1M4WF78XJPEJJ48D1JZ4SJ8/"
              class="moz-txt-link-freetext" moz-do-not-send="true">https://oprdvolunteerabuse.org/record/osp/01M1M4WF78XJPEJJ48D1JZ4SJ8/</a>,
            where it can be read today. Every prior production in my
            matters has been published the same way.</li>
        </ol>
        <br>
        <p>If any part of the fee is not waived, reduce it as ORS
          192.324(5) provides, state in writing for each of these five
          factors why it does or does not weigh in favor of a waiver,
          and give an itemized estimate by task and hourly rate before
